# `$81:990B`'s `$5D` path, which is the one branch in the family that reaches
# off its own page: `LDX $40 : CPX #$FFFF : BEQ +` and then `STA $000E,X`,
# clearing a *peer* record's `ACTOR_COLLIDE_ID` before the freeze runs.
#
# It needs the ice weapon and the type $1E actor at (921,794) on the same level,
# and `movies/level29-ice.zmv` already proved level 29 is the level that has
# both — it walks the whole map west with `$5D` to reach an `$81:D704`. This
# movie keeps the first 4,113 frames of that route, which is everything up to
# and including the pickup, and then stops going west:
#
#     4145   -          the last leg of the ice movie's route, dropped
#     4160   B          `$7E:1CBC` $0000 -> $0001 at 4162
#     4200   Down+Y
#
# The `B` is 481 frames after the pickup (`$7E:1CCE` $0000 -> $0099 at 3679),
# which clears the 460-frame lockout that movie had to pay for the same reason.
# What is different here is that it does not have to walk anywhere afterwards:
# the creature closes the 157 pixels itself, and the fight is a stand rather
# than a retreat.
#
# **It freezes at about 4290 and then nothing moves again.** The record's
# collision id goes `$03` -> `$36` and its position stops changing for the rest
# of the run — 924,630 on every sampled frame from 4300 on, and still 924,630
# 800 frames later if the movie is allowed to run — with the peer parked beside
# it at 909,615. That is why 4,600 frames is enough.
#
# What it buys:
#
#     d990b_freeze_peer  715
#     d990b_freeze_alone  10
#
# Ten is the interesting number, and it is ten because of thirteen frames.
# `--watch 0440` says `$40` holds `$FFFF` at 4200 and `$1A3E` from 4213, and
# never changes again for the rest of the run: the companion record does not
# exist when the shooting starts and is allocated once, thirteen frames in. So
# `d990b_freeze_alone` is not a rare case of a normal fight, it is the opening
# of one — and the only reason both sides of the branch are in the same movie is
# that the first shot was fired before the creature was finished assembling
# itself. Both sides of a test that only exists because this copy of `$81:8888`
# looks at another actor's page.
#
# The census is empty over this run: nothing staggers, so nothing asks for
# `$81:96E4`. `movies/level29-990b.zmv` is where that happens.

